WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.010
Every protein in your body is born as a straight thread and takes on a single correct shape within fractions of a second.

2
00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:13.700
But if it tried to find that shape by trial and error, the entire age of the universe so far would not be enough.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.950
So the chain is not really searching — then what is it doing?

4
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:32.560
Inside your cells, right at this moment, there is a loom running without pause.

5
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:41.970
This machine, called the ribosome, reads the genetic instruction letter by letter, and for every letter it reads, it dangles one bead from its tip.

6
00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.780
There are twenty kinds of bead: the amino acids.

7
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.430
They are strung one after another, in a single file.

8
00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:55.960
What comes off the loom is a flexible, straight thread, hundreds of beads long, resembling nothing at all.

9
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:01.530
At a rate of a few beads per second, thousands are produced every minute.

10
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.140
And it has to be said that this thread, on its own, is good for nothing.

11
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:11.870
As a straight thread, a protein is not an enzyme, not a transporter, not a channel.

12
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:13.920
It is nothing.

13
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.730
Because a protein's job comes from its shape.

14
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:24.740
Think of an enzyme: its task is to catch a particular molecule and either cut it or join it to something else.

15
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:30.630
To do that, its surface must carry a pocket that fits that molecule, and only that molecule.

16
00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:40.520
The depth of the pocket, its width, the placement of the charges along its walls, the pattern of how it pushes water away or draws it in — all of it must be finely tuned.

17
00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.770
That pocket emerges when the thread bends and folds back on itself.

18
00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:54.700
Two amino acids sitting perhaps forty beads apart in the sequence end up side by side once folding is complete, forming the two walls of that pocket.

19
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.910
Shape is function itself.

20
00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:01.200
The wrong shape is not a broken tool; it is no tool at all.

21
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.450
Here is the astonishing part: the thread finds this shape by itself.

22
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:19.220
There is no external mold, no press stamping the thread into the required form, no separate instruction file saying "now bend here." The moment the thread leaves the loom and is released into a watery environment,

23
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:26.710
it gathers itself up spontaneously and settles, almost every single time, into the same single three-dimensional structure.

24
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:37.240
And it does this very quickly: for small proteins the job is over somewhere between a millionth of a second and a thousandth of a second; for large ones it takes at most a few seconds.

25
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:43.610
The same sequence, today and tomorrow, in you and in a fish, always arrives at the same shape.

26
00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:49.020
The experiment that proved this really is how it works was done in nineteen sixty-one.

27
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.630
Christian Anfinsen was working on an enzyme called ribonuclease.

28
00:02:53.630 --> 00:03:03.200
This enzyme is one hundred twenty-four amino acids long, a small and sturdy protein, and its structure is additionally clamped together by four sulfur bridges.

29
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.850
Anfinsen first tore the enzyme down.

30
00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:13.380
With urea he destroyed the delicate balance holding the structure, and with a reducing agent he cut the four bridges.

31
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:19.750
What he had left in his hands was a slack, functionless, straight thread adrift in solution.

32
00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:24.360
The enzyme was dead — or rather, it had stopped being an enzyme.

33
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.490
Then Anfinsen removed those agents from the solution and waited.

34
00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.820
The thread pulled itself back together.

35
00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:37.550
The severed bridges were rebuilt, and ninety-four percent of the enzyme's activity came back.

36
00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:47.600
One detail here lifts the experiment out of being an ordinary story of recovery and turns it into decisive proof: the protein had eight sulfurs ready to form bridges,

37
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:53.010
and there are one hundred and five different ways to pair those eight sulfurs into four couples.

38
00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:58.900
If the bridges had formed at random, the chance of hitting the correct pattern would have been around one percent.

39
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.710
Yet the thread found the correct pattern almost every time.

40
00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:13.080
The conclusion that emerged became one of the cornerstones of molecular biology: all the information that determines a protein's final shape is already contained in its amino acid sequence.

41
00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:21.370
Given the sequence, the shape appears on its own, as the chain settles in water into the most stable, lowest-energy arrangement available.

42
00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:28.700
For this work, Anfinsen shared the nineteen seventy-two Nobel Prize in Chemistry with Stanford Moore and William Stein.

43
00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.310
It is also worth adding just how fragile the thing holding that shape up really is.

44
00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:36.800
There is no strong cement gripping a folded protein.

45
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:47.170
There are hydrogen bonds, there are weak electrical attractions, there is the mutual finding of positive and negative charges — but the most decisive factor is the behavior of water.

46
00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:52.900
Some of the amino acids are oil-like; they shy away from any contact with water.

47
00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:58.150
Rather than keeping them outside, water presses them inward in order to preserve its own ordering.

48
00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:06.600
Seen from the outside, the protein becomes a shell on friendly terms with water, while inside it becomes a tightly packed oily core.

49
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:14.410
Each one of these forces, taken individually, is comparable in size to the jostling the molecules suffer from ordinary heat.

50
00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:22.860
In other words, a protein stands up by the sum of a set of whispers, and most proteins are only a few whispers away from coming undone.

51
00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:31.310
Anfinsen had closed the question of "why this shape." But at that very moment, without realizing it, he opened a far more unsettling one.

52
00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:36.080
If the shape is written in the sequence, how does the thread find that shape?

53
00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:42.450
The person who turned this question into a calculation was the American molecular biologist Cyrus Levinthal.

54
00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:50.580
Levinthal belonged to the first generation to set computers running on biological questions; he was someone accustomed to thinking in numbers.

55
00:05:50.580 --> 00:06:01.270
At a scientific meeting he attended in nineteen sixty-nine, he asked the thing no one had been asking out loud: what is the thread actually doing while it finds the correct shape?

56
00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:04.600
The first answer that comes to mind is searching.

57
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.210
It tries out various shapes and stays with whichever is most stable.

58
00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.540
Levinthal worked out what that answer would cost.

59
00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:16.670
Along the backbone of a protein strand there are bonds that can rotate.

60
00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:25.440
At its own point of connection, every amino acid has two fundamental rotational freedoms — and that is before we count the twisting of the side chains.

61
00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:31.810
These rotations are not entirely free: atoms collide with one another, and certain angles are forbidden.

62
00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:39.620
But even if only a handful of distinct postures were available to each amino acid, those postures multiply as you move along the chain.

63
00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:48.390
For a medium-sized protein of one hundred fifty amino acids, Levinthal estimated the number of reachable shapes at roughly ten to the three hundredth power.

64
00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:54.120
It is not easy to grasp what that number means, because nothing in everyday life corresponds to it.

65
00:06:54.120 --> 00:07:00.010
The total count of all the atoms in the observable universe is somewhere around ten to the eightieth power.

66
00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:11.180
So if you took every atom in the universe and treated it as a separate universe, and then took every atom in each of those universes and treated it as a universe again, and repeated the trick three times over,

67
00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:15.150
you still would not reach ten to the three hundredth power.

68
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:20.080
That is the range of options standing in front of a single protein strand.

69
00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:21.970
Now let us add time.

70
00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:33.780
For a bond to swing around and settle into a new posture takes place on the scale of the fastest molecular motions physicists know of: roughly ten to the minus thirteenth power seconds — a few trillionths of a second.

71
00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:45.910
Even if the protein worked at that maximum speed, never stopping, never hesitating, never trying the same shape twice, exhausting every option would take ten to the two hundred eighty-seventh power seconds.

72
00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:56.440
The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old; converted into seconds, that is roughly ten to the seventeenth power seconds — a one followed by seventeen zeros.

73
00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:06.810
Take the ratio, and for a single small protein to finish working through its list of shapes you would need ten to the two hundred seventieth power times the lifetime of the universe.

74
00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:11.100
Set the sum up from the other end and the picture is unchanged.

75
00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:15.550
A protein finishes folding in fractions of a second.

76
00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:22.880
In that span, on the most generous estimate, the number of shapes it could try does not exceed a figure with eight zeros after it.

77
00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:31.810
This was precisely Levinthal's point: the ratio of the shapes a protein visits in its lifetime to the shapes available to it is indistinguishable from zero.

78
00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:36.420
The protein is not finishing the search; it is not even properly beginning it.

79
00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:41.990
This went down in history as Levinthal's paradox, though the name is slightly misleading.

80
00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:45.000
There is no logical contradiction here.

81
00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.170
What there is, is a refutation.

82
00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:53.260
If the assumption of blind search were correct, proteins could not fold.

83
00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:57.390
Since proteins do fold, the assumption is wrong.

84
00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:02.640
That was Levinthal's own conclusion as well: folding is not a search, it is a route.

85
00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.730
The strand is not wandering among shapes; it is following a particular path.

86
00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:18.420
Levinthal took this idea so seriously that he went as far as to suggest that the shape a protein settles into need not be the most stable shape at all — it might simply be the shape that is reachable.

87
00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:25.430
That stood in tension with Anfinsen's conclusion, and it was the seed of a dispute that split the field in two for years.

88
00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:31.640
What is more, as the experiments advanced, the paradox did not ease — it grew heavier.

89
00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:38.170
As measurement techniques sharpened, it became clear that proteins fold faster than anyone had supposed.

90
00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:44.540
Some small protein fragments finish the job on the scale of a microsecond, a millionth of a second.

91
00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:55.390
And at exactly this point the question outgrows the boundaries of biology and takes on a barer form: how does physics — which cannot see ahead, does not calculate,

92
00:09:55.390 --> 00:10:01.760
does not know the target — find the needle in an unimaginably vast haystack on its very first move?

93
00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.130
It was not the numbers that were wrong.

94
00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:07.300
What was wrong was the picture in our heads.

95
00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:17.350
The idea of a search quietly builds an image in the mind: a perfectly flat expanse, billions of equivalent points spread across it, and somewhere a single hole.

96
00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:27.080
Ken Dill likened this image to a gigantic golf course: wherever you place the ball, you get no clue at all about the hole, because the ground is at the same height everywhere.

97
00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:35.690
On a course like that, the only way to find the hole really is to try every point — and Levinthal's arithmetic holds flawlessly on such a course.

98
00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:41.260
The question is this: why should a protein's energy landscape be a golf course?

99
00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:48.110
In nineteen eighty-seven, Joseph Bryngelson and Peter Wolynes approached that question from an unexpected direction.

100
00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:55.920
They adapted the statistical mechanics of disordered magnetic materials — systems known as spin glasses — to the protein chain.

101
00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.570
The decisive concept in this approach is conflict.

102
00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:10.260
The amino acids within a chain can enter into many different pairings with one another at the same time, and some of those pairings exclude each other: if one is formed, the other cannot be.

103
00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:19.510
In a randomly written chain, conflict is abundant; such a chain gets stuck among hundreds of competing, half-stable states and can never commit to any of them.

104
00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:24.600
What Bryngelson and Wolynes saw was that natural proteins are not random chains.

105
00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.890
Evolution had selected sequences in which conflict is minimized.

106
00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:34.460
In a chain like that, almost all of the correct contacts reinforce one another.

107
00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:42.590
In nineteen ninety-two, Leopold, Montal and Onuchic captured what this property does to the landscape in a single word: a funnel.

108
00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:52.000
Not a flat playing field, but a broad funnel, sloping from its rim down toward the center, pitted and ridged along the way, with the correct structure sitting at the bottom.

109
00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:57.570
Here is the difference: in this landscape, the chain doesn't have to try anything at all.

110
00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:05.700
At every step, a slight energetic tilt nudges it downward, and each step taken downward sharply narrows the set of options that remain.

111
00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:10.790
A single contact formed correctly sweeps thousands of shapes off the table at once.

112
00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:18.280
A very small amount of guidance per step is enough to bring durations on the order of the lifetime of the universe down to microseconds.

113
00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:24.650
The protein isn't finding a needle in a haystack; everywhere in the haystack, the ground slopes toward the needle.

114
00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:28.300
The broad outlines of real folding fit this picture.

115
00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:38.830
First comes a collapse on the order of a millionth of a second: the amino acids that behave like oil flee inward under the pressure of water, and the chain, its volume shrunk several fold,

116
00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:42.960
turns into a tangle that is still loose but now gathered together.

117
00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:50.290
This intermediate state is called the molten globule; the shape is roughly right, the details haven't settled into place yet.

118
00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:56.820
Then, at the bottom of the funnel, the fine tuning begins, and the atoms lock into their final positions.

119
00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:00.950
That this picture is more than theory was shown in twenty eleven.

120
00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:02.840
David E.

121
00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:12.730
Shaw's team simulated twelve small, fast-folding proteins atom by atom on a special supercomputer called Anton, designed to compute nothing but molecular motion.

122
00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:21.820
Inside the computer, the chains folded on their own, unfolded, and folded again; the structures they arrived at matched the structures measured in the laboratory,

123
00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:25.790
and the times they took matched the times measured in the laboratory.

124
00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:33.120
What's more, the routes became visible: there is no single path, but a family of routes converging toward the bottom.

125
00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:37.890
Levinthal was right to speak of a path, but the path was not a single one.

126
00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:45.220
Even so, it would be wrong to declare a clean ending here, because the "protein folding problem" is really three separate questions.

127
00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:52.230
How does sequence determine shape; how does folding happen this fast; and can we compute shape from sequence.

128
00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:55.400
The last question has been solved.

129
00:13:55.400 --> 00:14:06.570
In twenty twenty, at the international structure prediction contest held every two years, an artificial intelligence system called AlphaFold produced structures with an accuracy close to experimental measurement,

130
00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:11.020
opening up a field that had been blocked for decades in a single stroke.

131
00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:21.230
Half of the twenty twenty-four Nobel Prize in Chemistry went to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper for protein structure prediction, and the other half to David Baker for protein design.

132
00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:28.560
But these systems work by reading the statistical traces evolution has left in the sequences of related proteins.

133
00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:32.530
They tell you the destination; they don't tell you the journey.

134
00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:38.580
Knowing where a protein will settle is not the same as knowing how, and how quickly, it gets there.

135
00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:43.350
The second question, Levinthal's question, is the part that remains open.

136
00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:47.960
The inside of a cell, moreover, doesn't resemble a test tube.

137
00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:56.250
The chain emerges from the ribosome not all at once but gradually, from one end to the other; folding begins before the sequence is even complete,

138
00:14:56.250 --> 00:15:02.140
which means the first half of the protein gives itself a shape without ever having seen the second half.

139
00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:06.270
The cell is so crowded that molecules sit right up against one another.

140
00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:16.800
For risky chains there are dedicated helper proteins; these enclose the newborn strand and insulate it from the outside world, opening up a sheltered room in which it can pull itself together.

141
00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:20.930
Why that help is needed is explained by the dark side of the funnel.

142
00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:26.820
Some of the dimples along the funnel's rim are so deep that a chain falling into one cannot get out.

143
00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:38.310
Misfolded proteins glue their exposed oily surfaces to one another and build long, stiff fibers; the deposits that accumulate in the brain in Alzheimer's and Parkinson's disease are of this kind.

144
00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:48.360
In prion diseases the situation is even more unsettling: a misfolded shape imposes its own shape on neighboring molecules, which is to say the error copies itself.

145
00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:56.650
In these examples, the protein's natural shape isn't even the most stable of the shapes available to it; it is merely the shape the chain reaches sooner.

146
00:15:56.650 --> 00:16:03.340
That contrarian intuition of Levinthal's found its counterpart, fifty years later, in the biology of disease.

147
00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:14.350
And on top of that, a significant fraction of human proteins contain regions that never settle into a stable shape and stay loose; these do their jobs precisely because they remain undefined.

148
00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:19.600
One sequence corresponds to one shape is not a law, but a good rule of thumb.

149
00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:23.410
Looking back, the thing that actually changed isn't the number.

150
00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:30.420
Levinthal's ten to the power of three hundred never got any smaller; it is still correct today.

151
00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:33.110
What changed is how we read that number.

152
00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:39.640
We had taken it for a calculation about time; in fact, it is a measure of what evolution was forced to do.

153
00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:44.730
A randomly written chain of amino acids doesn't fold, it clumps.

154
00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:55.260
The ability to fold isn't an intrinsic property of a chain; it is the privilege of special sequences, won through billions of years of selection, that have turned their landscape into a funnel.

155
00:16:55.260 --> 00:17:00.670
So the answer to the question "how is it this fast" is not a fast search.

156
00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:03.680
The answer is that there was never any search at all.

157
00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:07.170
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

158
00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:10.660
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
